1. What is the List interface? How does it differ from Set and Queue?
List extends Collection and represents an ordered sequence that maintains insertion order, allows duplicate elements,
allows multiple null values, and provides index-based (positional) access via methods like get(index), set(index, e), add(index, e).

vs Set: Set does not guarantee order (except LinkedHashSet, which preserves insertion order, and TreeSet, which sorts)
 and does not allow duplicates. List explicitly allows duplicates and always maintains order by index.
vs Queue: Queue is designed for holding elements prior to processing, typically in FIFO order (or priority order for PriorityQueue),
and elements are accessed from the head only (peek/poll) — not by arbitrary index. List allows random access to any position.

2. What are the classes that implement List?

ArrayList — resizable array-backed implementation, fast random access
LinkedList — doubly linked list, also implements Deque
Vector — legacy, synchronized, resizable array
Stack — extends Vector, provides LIFO (stack) operations
CopyOnWriteArrayList — thread-safe variant (java.util.concurrent) that copies the underlying array on every write

Note: Vector and Stack are legacy classes from pre-Java 2 that were retrofitted into the Collections Framework when it was introduced in Java 1.2.

3. Is List an ordered collection? Does it allow duplicates and null values?
Yes — List is ordered by insertion (index-based). It allows duplicate elements and allows multiple null values. Exception: immutable lists created via List.of() do not allow null and throw NullPointerException if you try to add one.

4. What is the difference between List and Array in Java?

Array: a fixed-size container, size is declared at creation and cannot change, can hold either primitives or objects directly.
List: an interface implemented by dynamically resizable classes (ArrayList, LinkedList, etc.), holds only objects (primitives get autoboxed, e.g., int → Integer).

Important nuance: only ArrayList is internally array-backed. List itself is just an interface and makes no guarantee about internal structure — LinkedList, for example, uses linked nodes, not an array.

5. How does ArrayList work internally?
ArrayList is backed internally by an Object[] array. Elements are stored in contiguous indices, which is what gives get(index) O(1) access. When the array reaches capacity and a new element is added, a new, larger array is allocated, and existing elements are copied over using System.arraycopy() — this resize operation is O(n), though insertions themselves are O(1) amortized over time.